\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 While Alexander was getting about his personal hobbies and vendettas, his promotional team had been up and down   the three or four mountain villages of the higher elevations, blocking as many shopping centre, Country Women\'92s Association, and National Socialist Network driveways as they could plausibly claim ignorance about, bringing the good news and free merch and free tickets to the greatest live event in the history of tabloid talk and execution shows.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 By the time Alexander\'92s eager young spruikers had handed out, tossed and shot from a compression cannon every last t-shirt, cap, fanny pack, coffee mug, home DNA-swab kit, last meal lunchbox, toy trebuchet, and Wheel of Death keyring, everyone who wanted a ticket had got one. The High Elvis got a tranche for himself and a small commando team. Gerry Hardman mugged a kid on a skateboard and stole one.\
Perhaps the most surprising acquisitor of a ticket was the fully recovered Haloren Smythe, thanks to thorough planning, the commitment of his own Agent Jones exfiltration team, and the dedication and greed of a white tent medical team now mostly deceased.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 At half past four, Alexander\'92s faction of Shakti Security accepted ticket holders through the pedestrian access of the gates, though the Lysander auditorium doors would not open for another hour. A crowd had been gathering since early afternoon between the compound and the UDF checkpoint a few hundred metres north, and had made a carnival of it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 The best Major Midler could ask for was that the road be kept clear, but even that was a logistical impossibility as pedestrians, cyclists, burger trucks, bars on wheels, flatbeds piled with Elvi merch\'85 were passed through the checkpoint. The last thing Midler wanted was to fire on civilians\'97that was a job for the police, but Midler\'92s political masters wanted to bring their army out to play.\
From his relocated command post in a dog-grooming salon, the shampoo-allergic Major Midler watched the gate and checkpoints through UDF feeds, as software ran security checks and displayed names and any nefarious history. The mission was a disaster from the moment the Elvi started showing up; they were known to get punchy over song lyrics, let alone orders to move on. Midler was clear there should be no escalation with essentially peaceful entertainers unless provoked. But Midler suspected that might be the point; he\'92d heard the rumours that PAPER was behind the recent viral outbreak among the Elvi; perhaps now PAPER were deliberately concocting the situation against Shakti as a cover for an overt assault on the Elvi. But those cockroaches wouldn\'92t step out into the light of day, so they had politicians position the UDF to do it for them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Underclass or not, Midler was not firing on civilians. He\'92d wait till the Candid Criminal show was over, until Sarah Jacobson had given birth, if necessary. But Midler assessed he wouldn\'92t need to wait that long. If he\'92d read Alexander Shakti right, he might just bring the whole place down on his own.
\f2\fs24 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Gerry Hardman milled among the crowd and watched as his crew joined the entry queue at well-spaced intervals. Each had dressed as Elvi tribute artists, complete with bouffant wig, cape and as many sequins and rhinestones as they could individually stand. During the planning and logistics of the operation they became known as Clown Squad, and the name stuck. None of the operatives seemed to mind. \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Gerry didn\'92t pass up the opportunity to watch Shakti security in action as pedestrians and vehicles were permitted  through two of the three passages of the gate assembly. Gerry noted how calmly and cordially\'97even jovially, as if they themselves had something to celebrate\'97they worked outside the pedestrian and vehicle access ways while the country\'92s defence force looked on. They were thorough; inspecting capes, jumpsuits, leather, sunglasses, pompadours, guitars, guitar cases and suspicious looking rhinestones. Gerry watched the first member of his Clown Squad hand over a ticket, open a guitar case, endure an inspection. He passed though and the membrane returned behind them.\
The construction of the fencing and gate assembly fascinated Gerry, and he could have known it protected Shakti property just by looking at it. Out of the scrub either side the fencing arrived with slender smoke-dark alloy tendrils sweeping up as if growing from the ground. They rose and loosely entwined like a vine, crossing and parting without ever quite resolving into bars, mesh or anything else a sensible intelligence officer could draw on a whiteboard. Apparently superimposed in the same three-dimensional space were flat triangular prisms that seemed to compel him to look away. If he held his breath and focussed, he thought he could see through the prisms, then realised that was not strictly true. The view of bush and road beyond seemed to have been subtly manipulated and passed through large panes of cracked smoky glass. He moved his head a little, and a metal tendril that appeared thick enough to stop a truck narrowed almost to wire, while another that had barely been there caught the light and became substantial.\
Gerry found it beautiful, even as he was forced to exhale and look away. He felt he was in a gallery full of abstract art; that degenerate stuff always made him want to puke. When he looked back he tried to observe the assembly obliquely without studying it.  \
The outbound vehicle gate was up, and it looked like a seamless continuation of the fence. It had no border or frame, no obvious hinge line, no wheel track, piston, motor housing or locking mechanism, and looked like a piece of sculptural calligraphy written exactly where vehicles needed to pass.\
The pedestrian and inbound vehicle panels of the assemblies were, if anything, even more inexplicable than the fence and the raised outbound gate. The planes that their gates would occupy when raised alternated between empty for passing through and a kind of smoky energetic barrier or membrane that Gerry immediately understood meant the opening was lethal. Yet vehicles and foot traffic were happily queuing and waiting without apparent thought to the possibility that the barrier might switch back on as they were passing, and cut them in half or turn them to cinders, or something ever grislier. Tax, Gerry was not looking forward to walking through there when the time came.\
Neither membrane looked thick enough to contain anything. As with the gates, the geometry of the road and bush beyond appeared unreliable, though easier to make out, because the barrier was flatter and lacked both the tendrils and the prismatic distortions of the fencing. The kerb beyond stepped sideways and up by a few centimetres. Legs appeared fractionally displaced beneath the people they belonged to. At times the membrane momentarily opaque until Gerry shifted his viewing angle.\
A guard took pedestrian tickets. Another searched for what people carried. Identities were checked on a device. The barrier across the pedestrian access disappeared and people were waved through, and they happily complied. The barrier reappeared. The guard checked the next tickets presented. The cycle repeated. Gerry wondered what controlled the state of the barriers\'97a manual operator in a control room somewhere, or some automated process. He hoped it wasn\'92t automated, even more so if the software had been certified to have consciousness. He sided with manual operation; Eloise Shakti would understand the technical details of all the gadgets she commanded, and Gerry doubted she\'92d trust automation over a decision to potentially fry or evaporate dinner guests.\
As Gerry watched, hundreds passed through a space whose concealed defences had been lethal only moments before\'97his own Clown Squad among them. He was tempted to chuck a stone at the vehicular barrier while there was no queue, just to test it, but he thought better of drawing attention to himself.\
The ticket guard sent one Elvis aside after a result came back on the screen. He protested that the matter involving a council fountain and six inflatable sheep had been dealt with years ago.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 A truck horn sounded back up the road. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Then another. Gerry heard cheering. The carnival throng shuffled off the road to the narrow verges, parted by a decrepit old flatbed stacked with speaker cabinets and a lighting truss. A banner was tied across its grill reading, 
\fs20 FREE CONCERT FOR THE HOLY MOMMA
\fs22 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Some seconds later, the crowd parted again, ceding road access to a convoy of vehicles driven by Elvi while more rode out back on equipment cases and leant against lighting rigging.\
Shakti Security stopped the first truck before the inbound membrane. The driver climbed down dressed in white leather and presented paperwork, which the guard read and queried.  The driver pointed at the stage equipment. The guard looked at the crowd. The driver pointed at the banner.\
Another Elvi got out from the passenger side and explained his attitude with both arms. The first guard called over a supervisor. The supervisor inspected the paperwork, then several of the trucks, then the crowd again as though hoping one of those things would cease to exist while she was looking elsewhere.\
Gerry moved no closer, but the crowd did that for him. The supervisor made a call. Word came down that the paperwork was probably fake, but Mr Shakti had just authorised it anyway. Perhaps he had ideas of fronting the band.\
Gerry almost smiled; the more distractions, the better. He pulled out his device and messaged his Clown Squad to reconnoitre the Elvi Concert set-up.\
Gerry approached a Shakti guard. \'93We\'92re getting a concert too?\'94\
The guard shrugged. \'93I guess so. First I\'92ve heard of it. Mr Shakti is giving it as a present to his missus.\'94\
Shakti security swarmed over the convoy; Gerry gave them an A+ for thoroughness and ingenuity. These guys were alert and had been trained well.\
Gerry watched the membrane over in the inbound vehicle lane evaporate, and kept watching it until the last Elvis truck had passed through. The guards inspected everything: road cases, speakers, amplifiers, lighting racks, everything. As each vehicle was cleared, the membrane evaporated, then reappeared within centimetres of its rear bumper as it passed through. Gerry would not like to risk trying to walk in alongside a cleared vehicle.\
The last vehicle was a van with 
\fs20 CAKE, BAGEL & ROLL CATERING 
\fs22 on the side. It had joined the queue late, and  the guards found only three guitar cases inside, which piqued Gerry\'92s interest. He almost wanted to take a look himself, but he had other objectives. Anyway, the guards had the cases opened and the guitars taken out. They even had the driver shake the acoustic guitar to check nothing had been pushed inside. The guitar rattled, and it took several minutes before anyone could shake a single guitar pick out of it. The Elvis thanked the guard; that pick had been driving him crazy for weeks.
